## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
3D Morton codes enable fast spatial indexing in grid-like domains; a branchless, fixed-bit spreading approach scales predictably. 

## Key Concepts
- Morton codes for 3D require interleaving bits from three coordinates.
- Bit spreading (dilating) inserts two zero bits between each original bit; a fixed sequence of masks/shifts yields a branchless implementation.
- BigInt vs. Number in JavaScript: use BigInt for 64-bit results if implementing in JS; otherwise 64-bit types in C/C++ handle it natively.

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n) {
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn; // 21 bits
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n)) & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n)) & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n)) & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x, y, z) {
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness for random inputs? 
- How to adapt to larger coordinates or alternative word sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of constant-time bit manipulation and branchless programming, which are critical in performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts

- Trailing zero count algorithms
- De Bruijn sequences for bit indexing
- Branchless bit-twiddling techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
// Conceptual implementation
function ctz64(x) {
  if (x === 0) return 64;
  const y = x & -x;
  const idx = table[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89) >>> 58];
  return idx;
