## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 64-bit field layouts and perform cross-field moves without branches, a common pattern in graphics and encoding pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masking for 16-bit lanes
- Cross-lane permutation with shifts
- No loops/branches; constant-time operations

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t swapR_B(uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t r = v & 0x000000000000FFFFULL;
  uint64_t g = v & 0x00000000FFFF0000ULL;
  uint64_t b = v & 0x0000FFFF00000000ULL;
  uint64_t a = v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL;
  return (r << 32) | g | (b >> 32) | a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to swap G and A as well? 
- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crossing-boundary bit extraction on a 128-bit value with no branching, mirroring real-world crypto/low-level data parsing tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-boundary bit manipulation.
- Mask generation for widths up to 64 without 64-bit overflow.
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves (no loops).

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractBits128(hi, lo, pos, width) {
  const V = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const mask = width === 64 ? (1n << 64n) - 1n : (1n << BigInt(width)) - 1n;
  return (V >> BigInt(pos)) & mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of 128-bit bitboards, branchless kth-one, and cross-half boundary without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; popcount and select in constant time
- Branchless kth-one within a 64-bit word
- Correct handling when n is out of range across halves

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int findNthOne128(uint128 a, unsigned n){
  unsigned loCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.lo);
  unsigned hiCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.hi);
  unsigned total = loCnt + hiCnt;
  if (n >= total) return -1;
  if (n < loCnt) return kth1_64(a.lo, n);
  return 64 + kth1_64(a.hi, n - loCnt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 256-bit values without branches?
- What are trade-offs across compilers for portability vs performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?
